          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Working with Fractions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.086,
            0.596,
            0.15
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_1.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } \\times 5 } { 7 \\times 2 4 } = \\frac { 1 \\times 5 } { 7 \\times 2 } = \\frac { 5 } { 1 4 }"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.157,
            0.781,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us use the same technique to do one more multiplication."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.459,
            0.188,
            0.54,
            0.226
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac { 1 4 } { 1 5 } \\times \\frac { 2 5 } { 4 2 }"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.404,
            0.233,
            0.591,
            0.302
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_4.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{5}{3} = \\frac{1 \\times 5}{3 \\times 3} = \\frac{5}{9}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.308,
            0.849,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When multiplying fractions, we can first divide the numerator and denominator by their common factors before multiplying the numerators and denominators. This is called cancelling the common factors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.401,
            0.381,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Pinch of History"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.432,
            0.848,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In India, the process of reducing a fraction to its lowest terms — known as apavartana — is so well known that it finds mention even in a non-mathematical work. A Jaina scholar Umasvati (c. 150 CE) used it as a simile in a philosophical work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.515,
            0.293,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.548,
            0.849,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
